WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.106
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.406 --> 00:00:08.688
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.988 --> 00:00:16.227
Unit four: International Law. Today you will describe legal procedures and obligations with modal passives.

4
00:00:16.527 --> 00:00:19.051
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.152
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:24.017 --> 00:00:24.739
Treaty.

7
00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:28.829
Treaty means a formal written agreement between states.

8
00:00:29.417 --> 00:00:31.614
The treaty banned the use of landmines.

9
00:00:32.217 --> 00:00:34.405
Your turn. Say: Treaty.

10
00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:38.059
Treaty.

11
00:00:38.817 --> 00:00:39.795
Sovereignty.

12
00:00:40.783 --> 00:00:44.617
Sovereignty means a state's supreme authority over its territory.

13
00:00:45.217 --> 00:00:47.959
Intervention may violate national sovereignty.

14
00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.075
Your turn. Say: Sovereignty.

15
00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:55.155
Sovereignty.

16
00:00:55.917 --> 00:00:57.011
Jurisdiction.

17
00:00:58.017 --> 00:01:01.514
Jurisdiction means the official power to make legal decisions.

18
00:01:02.117 --> 00:01:04.906
The court has jurisdiction over war crimes.

19
00:01:05.517 --> 00:01:08.517
Your turn. Say: Jurisdiction.

20
00:01:11.367 --> 00:01:12.530
Jurisdiction.

21
00:01:13.283 --> 00:01:15.004
International tribunal.

22
00:01:16.017 --> 00:01:21.174
International tribunal means a court set up to judge cases under international law.

23
00:01:21.783 --> 00:01:24.468
The tribunal heard testimony from survivors.

24
00:01:25.083 --> 00:01:28.432
Your turn. Say: International tribunal.

25
00:01:31.267 --> 00:01:33.046
International tribunal.

26
00:01:33.783 --> 00:01:35.168
Diplomatic immunity.

27
00:01:36.183 --> 00:01:40.725
Diplomatic immunity means legal protection for diplomats from local prosecution.

28
00:01:41.317 --> 00:01:43.978
Diplomatic immunity prevented his arrest.

29
00:01:44.583 --> 00:01:47.828
Your turn. Say: Diplomatic immunity.

30
00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:52.330
Diplomatic immunity.

31
00:01:53.083 --> 00:01:54.479
Human rights law.

32
00:01:55.483 --> 00:01:59.039
Human rights law means law protecting the basic rights of all people.

33
00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:01.731
Human rights law prohibits torture.

34
00:02:02.317 --> 00:02:05.201
Your turn. Say: Human rights law.

35
00:02:08.067 --> 00:02:09.579
Human rights law.

36
00:02:10.317 --> 00:02:11.562
Ratification.

37
00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:16.198
Ratification means formal approval that makes an agreement binding.

38
00:02:16.783 --> 00:02:19.410
Ratification requires a vote in parliament.

39
00:02:20.017 --> 00:02:23.203
Your turn. Say: Ratification.

40
00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:27.312
Ratification.

41
00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:29.701
Enforcement mechanism.

42
00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:34.864
Enforcement mechanism means a means of making sure rules are obeyed.

43
00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:37.809
The treaty lacks an enforcement mechanism.

44
00:02:38.417 --> 00:02:41.928
Your turn. Say: Enforcement mechanism.

45
00:02:44.767 --> 00:02:46.673
Enforcement mechanism.

46
00:02:47.417 --> 00:02:48.302
Precedent.

47
00:02:49.317 --> 00:02:53.151
Precedent means an earlier decision used as a guide for later cases.

48
00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:55.854
The ruling set an important precedent.

49
00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:58.835
Your turn. Say: Precedent.

50
00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:02.655
Precedent.

51
00:03:03.417 --> 00:03:04.418
Statute.

52
00:03:05.417 --> 00:03:09.216
Statute means a written law or founding document of a court.

53
00:03:09.817 --> 00:03:12.281
The Rome Statute created the ICC.

54
00:03:12.883 --> 00:03:15.350
Your turn. Say: Statute.

55
00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:19.062
Statute.

56
00:03:19.867 --> 00:03:23.529
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:24.383 --> 00:03:29.416
Visal, Cambodia signed the Ottawa Treaty banning landmines. Does signing make it binding?

58
00:03:30.517 --> 00:03:37.964
Not on its own. Treaties must be ratified before they can be enforced, usually by a vote in the national parliament.

59
00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:41.491
And what happens if a state breaks a treaty?

60
00:03:42.583 --> 00:03:53.665
That's the weakness. Violations can be condemned, but sanctions can only be imposed if powerful states agree, so enforcement is often limited.

61
00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.947
Are there any successful examples?

62
00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:07.982
The Khmer Rouge Tribunal. Senior leaders were prosecuted under international and Cambodian law, which showed that impunity could be challenged.

63
00:04:09.133 --> 00:04:10.657
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:13.876
Grammar focus: Passive with modals.

65
00:04:14.176 --> 00:04:15.363
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:18.944
Treaties must be ratified before they can be enforced.

67
00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:23.138
Sanctions can only be imposed if powerful states agree.

68
00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:27.385
Violations should be investigated by an independent body.

69
00:04:28.333 --> 00:04:40.007
Legal English relies on modal passives: must be ratified (obligation), may be challenged (permission/possibility), shall be (formal rule in treaties). 'Can only be … if' states a strict condition.

70
00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:44.935
Be careful! Don't say: Treaties must ratified first.

71
00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:49.712
Say: Treaties must be ratified first.

72
00:04:50.012 --> 00:04:52.755
Modal passive: must be + p.p.

73
00:04:53.567 --> 00:04:56.115
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:58.714
Law Without a World Government.

75
00:04:59.014 --> 00:05:05.205
International law governs relations between states, yet there is no world police force to enforce it.

76
00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:11.032
Treaties can only be applied once they have been signed and ratified by national governments.

77
00:05:12.033 --> 00:05:18.294
Because states value their sovereignty, they are often reluctant to accept the jurisdiction of international courts.

78
00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:25.816
Nevertheless, tribunals such as the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia have shown that leaders can be held accountable.

79
00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:33.896
International law therefore depends less on force than on reputation, reciprocity and the slow building of precedent.

80
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.816
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:41.766
The agreement must blank by parliament.

82
00:05:42.066 --> 00:05:46.439
A, ratify. B, be ratified. C, been ratified.

83
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:52.557
The answer is B: be ratified.

84
00:05:53.717 --> 00:05:57.121
A state's supreme authority over its territory is blank.

85
00:05:57.421 --> 00:06:01.876
A, sovereignty. B, precedent. C, jurisdiction.

86
00:06:05.433 --> 00:06:07.340
The answer is A: sovereignty.

87
00:06:08.483 --> 00:06:11.249
An earlier ruling used as a guide is a blank.

88
00:06:11.549 --> 00:06:15.226
A, statute. B, treaty. C, precedent.

89
00:06:18.767 --> 00:06:20.685
The answer is C: precedent.

90
00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:23.757
Which collocation is correct?

91
00:06:24.057 --> 00:06:28.476
A, impose sanctions. B, make sanctions on. C, do sanctions.

92
00:06:32.033 --> 00:06:34.613
The answer is A: impose sanctions.

93
00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:39.387
According to the reading, international law depends largely on…

94
00:06:39.687 --> 00:06:46.219
A, a world police force. B, reputation, reciprocity and precedent. C, military power only.

95
00:06:49.767 --> 00:06:53.473
The answer is B: reputation, reciprocity and precedent.

96
00:06:54.667 --> 00:06:58.039
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:06:58.883 --> 00:07:01.870
Why is international law difficult to enforce?

98
00:07:18.683 --> 00:07:20.218
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:20.518 --> 00:07:58.392
International law is difficult to enforce mainly because there is no global authority with the power to compel sovereign states. Treaties can be signed with good intentions, but they may be ignored if a powerful country decides that its interests come first. For example, sanctions can only be imposed if major powers agree in the Security Council. On the other hand, the Khmer Rouge Tribunal showed that justice is possible, even if slow. So I think international law is weak, but far from meaningless.

100
00:07:58.692 --> 00:08:00.366
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:05.296
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and passive with modals.

102
00:08:05.596 --> 00:08:09.792
Today's value is justice. Stand up for what is fair for everyone.

103
00:08:10.092 --> 00:08:12.710
See you in Unit five. Goodbye!

104
00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:15.244
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

